## Problem

Search already scores a funder as the maximum `word_similarity(query, canonical_name)` or `word_similarity(query, alias)`, applies the inclusive request threshold, limits MPs and funders independently, then merges both ranked lists. The service currently drops scores and source names, so HTTP cannot explain why an alias found a canonical funder. GiST indexes need an indexable trigram operator predicate to participate; merely indexing columns under the existing function/aggregate predicates does not make those predicates indexable. Preserve search ordering, threshold inclusivity (including 0 and 1), per-kind limits, and canonical-name tie preference while exposing a typed match source.

## Usage (caller's view)

The service returns ranked results whose entity remains the canonical identity and whose source is explicit:

```rust
let results: Vec<EntitySearchResult> = search.search_entities(&request).await?;
for result in results {
    match &result.source {
        MatchSource::FunderAlias(alias) => show_alias_hint(result.entity.name(), alias),
        MatchSource::CanonicalName | MatchSource::MemberName => {}
    }
}
```

Repository result construction is similarly explicit:

```rust
let funders: Vec<ScoredFunder> = funder_repo.search_funders(&request).await?;
// Each row carries canonical Funder, exact winning score, and winning source.
```

The HTTP conversion emits an alias source only when the winning alias score is strictly greater than the canonical score. The browser parses the source as a closed discriminated union and shows `Matched alias: …` below the canonical result link only for the alias variant.

